# gitleaks findings that are ACCEPTED, by exact fingerprint
# (<commit>:<file>:<rule>:<line>). One line per finding; nothing else is
# suppressed.
#
# Why this file exists, and why the fix that made it necessary was incomplete.
#
# `skills.lock` used to store a BARE 64-hex digest per skill. gitleaks'
# generic-api-key rule fires on a keyword-bearing key beside a high-entropy
# value, and exactly one lock line supplies the keyword:
# "cms-platform/cms-platform-secrets". agentskills#87 fixed that AT THE SOURCE —
# the generator now writes `sha256:<hex>`, and `:` falls outside the rule's
# capture class, so the rule cannot fire on the CURRENT file. The blanket
# `[[allowlists]]` entry that used to suppress the bare-hex line shape was
# deleted with that change.
#
# What that missed: git history is immutable. `secrets-scan.yml` scans
# `base..head` on a pull request but THE FULL REACHABLE HISTORY on push,
# schedule and workflow_dispatch. Labelling the current lock cannot relabel the
# commit that introduced the bare-hex one, so deleting the allowlist turned
# every push to main red — while the PR that deleted it passed, because the PR
# lane never scanned the offending commit. The PR's own green `scan` was not
# evidence about the lane that actually breaks.
#
# A fingerprint is the right instrument here rather than restoring the blanket
# allowlist: it accepts exactly one finding in one historical commit, and
# leaves the rule live everywhere else — including on any FUTURE bare-hex lock
# line, which would be a real defect (it would mean the generator stopped
# labelling) and must still fail.
#
# Verify a change to this file with a `workflow_dispatch` run of
# `secrets-scan.yml` on the branch — that path scans full history. A pull
# request's own scan cannot test it.
